cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this repository or its results, please cite the paper below."
title: "Dynamical spectral functions from bitstring-sampled quantum subspaces: entanglement, not one-body magic, tracks the sampling cost"
authors:
  - family-names: "Bonilla Vargas"
    given-names: "Nicolás"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-6155-4391"
    affiliation: "Universidad Nacional de Colombia; SRH University Heidelberg/Munich; Daita AI"
    email: "ngbonillav@unal.edu.co"
abstract: >-
  Sample-based quantum diagonalization is lifted from the ground-state energy to dynamical spectral
  functions A(w), A(k,w), S(q,w) and S^zz(q,w), reconstructed from purely bitstring-sampled quantum
  subspaces with no Hadamard or controlled-unitary tests. The one-body fermionic magic F1 = 4 tr[g(1-g)]
  = 2Nu is shown to be an orbital-rotation invariant that is provably decoupled from the sampling cost |S|,
  which is instead lower-bounded and empirically tracked by the basis-dependent entanglement (bond
  dimension chi). Validated against exact diagonalization across Hubbard chains and a nineteen-molecule
  suite, and demonstrated on the IBM Heron processor.
